  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Colon cancer is a treatable, often curable malignancy beginning in the large intestine, typically starting as small, noncancerous polyps that grow over time. Key symptoms include persistent changes in bowel habits, rectal bleeding, unexplained weight loss, and fatigue. Early screening, generally starting at age 45, is critical for detecting polyps before they become cancerous. Early Signs and Symptoms Changes in Bowel Habits: Diarrhea, constipation, or narrowing of the stool lasting more than a few days. Rectal Bleeding: Bright red blood or very dark/black, tarry stool. Abdominal Discomfort: Persistent cramps, gas, pain, or a feeling that the bowel does not empty completely. Systemic Changes: Unexplained weight loss, weakness, fatigue, or anemia. Note: Many polyps or early-stage cancers do not produce symptoms, making screening vital. Stages of Colorectal CancerCancer staging is determined by the TNM system (tumor, node, metastasis): Stage 0: Carcinoma in situ (very early, localized to the inner lining). Stage I & II: Cancer has grown into the muscle layers of the colon wall but has not spread outside the colon. Stage III: Cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes. Stage IV: Metastatic cancer, having spread to distant organs like the liver or lungs. Risk Factors Age: Risk increases, particularly after age 45 or 50. Family History: Inherited conditions like Lynch syndrome or familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP). Lifestyle Factors: High consumption of red/processed meats, lack of exercise, obesity, smoking, and excessive alcohol use. Prognosis and TreatmentTreatment includes surgery (often curative if caught early), chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy. The 5-year survival rate is high—around 91% for localized cancer—but drops significantly to around 15.7% if the cancer has spread to distant parts of the body. PreventionRegular screenings, such as a colonoscopy, are the most effective method for prevention as they allow for the identification and removal of polyps before they turn into cancer.",
